{
  "case_id": "2001-01-463",
  "document_hash": "a742e6baafb3f0ebff2f7aef18ef762dc0dd8d19ea6305fc50ee430b2918a57c",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "EILEEN J. O'CONNOR",
      "case_id": "2001-01-463",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a742e6baafb3f0ebff2f7aef18ef762dc0dd8d19ea6305fc50ee430b2918a57c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a742e6baafb3-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. O'CONNOR: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: This case is about the authority of the Commissioner of Internal Revenue to assess FICA taxes that Congress has imposed on employers. It also involves the well-established principle of tax litigation. It involves two key sections of the Internal Revenue Code and how they relate to each other, and it involves the evidentiary value of assessment in tax litigation. The first of the key sections is section 6201. It appears at page 62a of the appendix to our petition. It authorizes and requires the Secretary of the Treasury to make inquiries, determinations, and assessments of all taxes imposed by the Internal Revenue Code. The second key section is 3111. It appears at page 55a of the appendix to our petition. This section imposes a tax on employers. The tax is measured by the wages they pay or are deemed to have paid their employees."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2001-01-463",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a742e6baafb3f0ebff2f7aef18ef762dc0dd8d19ea6305fc50ee430b2918a57c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a742e6baafb3-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Which section is this, Ms. O'Connor?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "EILEEN J. O'CONNOR",
      "case_id": "2001-01-463",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a742e6baafb3f0ebff2f7aef18ef762dc0dd8d19ea6305fc50ee430b2918a57c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a742e6baafb3-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. O'CONNOR: It doesn't need to, because under 3121(q) -- it's a very interesting provision. 3121 provides definitions, that's all. It resides in the subchapter of the Internal Revenue Code that provides for these employment taxes, and (q) is the definitional provision that says that tips are wages. It also tells you when the tips are wages. For the purposes of the employer FICA 3121(q) tells you that the wages are deemed to have been paid when the employee reports them or if the employer -- employee doesn't report them, they are wages deemed to be paid when the IRS issues notice of demand, so until the IRS notifies the restaurant that there is an additional FICA tax due, there is no interest or penalty."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-463",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a742e6baafb3f0ebff2f7aef18ef762dc0dd8d19ea6305fc50ee430b2918a57c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a742e6baafb3-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Where would the taxpayer get the information? The only thing the taxpayer has got are the employee's returns to the taxpayer and your assessment. Where is the taxpayer going to get the information that would allow it to do what you say in theory it could do?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. O'Connor",
      "case_id": "2001-01-463",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a742e6baafb3f0ebff2f7aef18ef762dc0dd8d19ea6305fc50ee430b2918a57c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a742e6baafb3-0001-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The 45B credit, as you point out, is available only for tips that are not used by the employer to satisfy his minimum wage obligations, yet it's not a complete wash to the Treasury, however, because you still have the FICA tax being paid in and an income tax credit being given, so it's the trust fund accounting that would have a problem if you look at it as a complete offset."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-463",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a742e6baafb3f0ebff2f7aef18ef762dc0dd8d19ea6305fc50ee430b2918a57c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a742e6baafb3-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: The assessment is presumptively correct, isn't it, under the statute?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Power",
      "case_id": "2001-01-463",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a742e6baafb3f0ebff2f7aef18ef762dc0dd8d19ea6305fc50ee430b2918a57c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a742e6baafb3-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. POWER: May it please the Court, Your Honor: Congress did not saddle the employer with a tax while depriving him of any way to defend against it. Congress did not require the employer to do what for 30 years it told him it was not required to do. This tax is not authorized. Because it's not authorized, that affects the burden of proof and the presumption of correctness."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Power",
      "case_id": "2001-01-463",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a742e6baafb3f0ebff2f7aef18ef762dc0dd8d19ea6305fc50ee430b2918a57c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a742e6baafb3-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. POWER: The employer does not know how the tips, the credit card tips are distributed. It's not as if --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2001-01-463",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a742e6baafb3f0ebff2f7aef18ef762dc0dd8d19ea6305fc50ee430b2918a57c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a742e6baafb3-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Congress has prohibited us from having that information. We do not have that information. We have never had that information. Congress for 30 years has told us we do not have to concern ourselves with that information, and we do not have the wherewithal to dispute it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-463",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a742e6baafb3f0ebff2f7aef18ef762dc0dd8d19ea6305fc50ee430b2918a57c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a742e6baafb3-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: But your position is, we know that there is a disparity between what is reported and what is actually paid in tips. We know that both the employee and the employer independently owe a tax on that total amount, and we know about the shortfall, but there is nothing the Government can do. It's just stuck by what the servers put down on the monthly form that they file."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2001-01-463",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a742e6baafb3f0ebff2f7aef18ef762dc0dd8d19ea6305fc50ee430b2918a57c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a742e6baafb3-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "No, it's entirely different, because -- and as the McQuatters case that's cited in the briefs by both parties illustrates, in that circumstance, the individual waiter has an opportunity to bring any defenses that he has to the Government's assessment forward, and we are denied every possible defense by that scenario."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-463",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a742e6baafb3f0ebff2f7aef18ef762dc0dd8d19ea6305fc50ee430b2918a57c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a742e6baafb3-0002-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: How? MS. POWER: -- that the employer would have to owe, because if any of that credit card tips that you're assuming isn't in fact a tip was received by somebody who made less than $20 a month, the employer doesn't owe any tax on that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2001-01-463",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a742e6baafb3f0ebff2f7aef18ef762dc0dd8d19ea6305fc50ee430b2918a57c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a742e6baafb3-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The burden of presumption and the burden of proof that they have put forth is premised upon the understanding that the person with that burden has the records and is in the best position to respond, and that does not characterize this situation, so those policy considerations behind that are not existing here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-463",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a742e6baafb3f0ebff2f7aef18ef762dc0dd8d19ea6305fc50ee430b2918a57c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a742e6baafb3-0002-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Ms. O'CONNOR, at the very outset you said that 311 imposed a tax on wages paid or deemed to have been paid. Is the word deemed in the statute?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2001-01-463",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a742e6baafb3f0ebff2f7aef18ef762dc0dd8d19ea6305fc50ee430b2918a57c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a742e6baafb3-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: How long has IRS been making these assessments, please, Ms. O'Connor?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2001-01-463",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a742e6baafb3f0ebff2f7aef18ef762dc0dd8d19ea6305fc50ee430b2918a57c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a742e6baafb3-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: In view of all the questions about the proper computation, why has the Government resisted notice and comment rulemaking to come up with a fair formula, because the contention is that the Government's formula exaggerates the income, exaggerates the tip income."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2001-01-463",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a742e6baafb3f0ebff2f7aef18ef762dc0dd8d19ea6305fc50ee430b2918a57c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a742e6baafb3-0003-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the contention is that the Government's formula exaggerates the income, exaggerates the tip income."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2001-01-463",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a742e6baafb3f0ebff2f7aef18ef762dc0dd8d19ea6305fc50ee430b2918a57c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a742e6baafb3-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: But why not -- why not employ that fair procedure of going through the notice and comment, and then -- I think that's what Judge Kozinski said to do."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2001-01-463",
